  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe system that brought yesterday evening's severe weather\nepisode is continuing to depart the area as the cold front has\nshifted well south of the forecast area. The next system of note\nis right on its heels in the form of a closed mid to upper low\nover the Four Corners region. This feature will move eastward\nthrough the morning, bringing enhanced upper-level divergence\nover the region. Renewed surface low development is expected,\nincreasing widespread rain and thunderstorm chances. Given \nour areas position on the cool side of the boundary, severe \nweather is not anticipated with any thunderstorm activity today.\nThe surface will be stable with temperatures mainly in the 50s \nacross the area, though some 60s are possible along the KS/OK \nborder. Rainfall will continue into early Thursday morning. \nRegarding temperatures today and tomorrow, have continued the \nidea of greatly lowering high temperatures compared to the NBM\noutput. It continues to struggle with the shallow cold, post \nfrontal airmass.\n\nTemperatures are expected to remain rather cool for the\nremainder of the week with high temperatures in the 50s and 60s\nand lows in the 50s dropping into the 30s and 40s by the end of\nthe week. The potential for frost does exist for Thursday night\ninto Friday morning, though cloud cover may be a limiting\nfactor. Western and northern zones may have the best chance at\nfrost development, but confidence is low at this time. Friday\nnight into Saturday morning has the best chance to see freezing\ntemperatures. Current forecast calls for widespread 30s, with\nbelow freezing west of Route 283. Probabilities of temperatures\n32 or less are 50-70%. Unless things change drastically before\nthen, would guess Frost/Freeze headlines being needed.",
